  </Configuration>
  <Item id="42005c0e-177c-533c-b041-731405482e73" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Welcome</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+40%">Governance: who may do what — and the proof of it</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>This section adds a governance layer to the AgriPTO safety case you have been reading. In plain words: the project now says who may approve, seal, edit and change its rules; every such action is signed with a key that belongs to a named person; every change of who-may-what is itself a signed record; and a single command (Verify all seals) re-derives all of it from the files in the repository and tells you what does and does not hold. Nothing is hidden in a server or a database: the policy, the keys, the waivers, the delegations and the activity log are files under .safesuite/, versioned with the documents, readable from any clone, verifiable offline.</Paragraph><Paragraph>The rows below go feature by feature. Each says what the feature is for someone who has never heard of it, where it lives in the application, what to try (and as whom — the cast's identities ship with the project), what you should see, and what it proves. The records were laid on <Text bold="true">2026-09-05</Text> by the same product code the application runs, as ordinary signed commits on top of the project's history: nothing was regenerated, and every hash, signature and chain link verifies.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Menus used throughout: Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ (Governance policy…, Policy waivers…, Pending signatures…, Delegations…, Verify all seals, Activity log, Team server settings…, Export evidence pack…); Document ▸ (Send for review…, Approve…, Reopen for editing…); Help ▸ Signing identity…; right-click a folder or document in the project explorer ▸ Show Effective Policy Here…; View ▸ Versions for the Baselines and Activity log tabs.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Scopes and paths are written from the project: the folder 5_Software is Model/5_Software, the HARA is Model/2_Concept/HARA.strdoc.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Governance/Verify_all_seals.txt is the report as it stood right after sealing; countdowns and pending states age from that date.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="d190b86d-bd91-52cf-9313-58cc91d37798" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Become someone</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">1 · Become a member of the cast</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Governed actions are refused to whoever holds no right to them — including you, as the Windows account you are logged in as, which the policy does not name. So the first step is to become one of the people it does name. Each member of the cast ships with a <Text bold="true">Level 1 signing identity</Text> (a passphrase-protected key file plus its identity record) under Governance/Identities/. The passphrase of every pack is <Text bold="true">agripto</Text>.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Open a terminal in the Governance folder and run  become.cmd elena-vidal  (or any folder name under Identities; become.cmd -List shows them).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Reopen Help ▸ Signing identity: 'This computer signs as Elena Vidal — Level 1 (local-passphrase)'. A running SafeSuite uses the new identity at its next signed action; reopening the project is the safe way.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Switching person = running become.cmd again. become.cmd -Restore puts your own identity back (it was saved aside the first time).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Every signature asks for the passphrase. To stop the prompts for a while, use Session unlock in the same dialog (row 3).</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph>These keys are fictional and worth nothing outside this demonstration. What matters is that each is a real key: the signatures it makes verify against the public half that the project's trust records hold, and against nothing else.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="ef72bc3f-2654-5db9-b117-eac667ed9fd3" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>The cast</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">2 · The cast, and what each may do</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>The policy names the team as it already worked on the AgriPTO case. Who holds what:</Paragraph><Table><Column width="110.00"/><Column width="120.00"/><Column width="260.00"/><Column width="70.00"/><Column width="150.00"/><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Center"><Text bold="true">Person</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Center"><Text bold="true">Role in the project</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Center"><Text bold="true">Policy grant</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Center"><Text bold="true">Key</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Center"><Text bold="true">Vetting</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Elena Vidal</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Safety manager</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Owner (everything, project-wide); holds the trust root</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>root; CLMS-linked</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Ravi Patel</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Project manager</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Owner; created the policy, handed the root to Elena</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>self-enrolled (was the first root)</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Greta Hoffmann</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Independent assessor</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Assessor: read, approve, sign baselines, export evidence</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>site-manager-vetted</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Henrik Larsen</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Systems lead</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Author + Approver in Model/3_System</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>site-manager-vetted</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Claire Moreau</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Software lead</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Author + Approver in Model/5_Software</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>site-manager-vetted, citing her CLMS seat</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Paolo Ferraro</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Verification lead</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Author + Reviewer in Model/6_Verification</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>site-manager-vetted</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Tomasz Kowal</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Hardware engineer</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Author in Model/4_Hardware</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>self-enrolled</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Callum Reid</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Contract firmware engineer</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Author in Model/5_Software, until 2026-09-15</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>L1, pack</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>self-enrolled</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Νίκος Παπαδόπουλος</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>External reviewer</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Reviewer in Model/6_Verification; must sign at Level 2 or above</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>no key yet</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>—</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>田中 優子</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Reader</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Reader project-wide, except Model/AI-Demos</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>no key yet</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>—</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row><Row><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Дмитрий Соколов</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Summer contractor</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>Author in Model/6_Verification, 1 Jun – 31 Aug 2026 (lapsed)</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>revoked</Text></Paragraph></Cell><Cell><Paragraph alignment="Left"><Text>—</Text></Paragraph></Cell></Row></Table><Paragraph>Roles are editable presets, not a fixed list: Assessor and Project manager were added for this team; Owner, Author, Reviewer, Approver and Reader are the defaults. A grant is a person, roles, an optional folder scope, optional carve-outs, an optional minimum-tier override and an optional validity window. Rights add up across grants; nothing takes a right away except a grant's own end.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="5eda24c1-1bb3-50f7-9bf1-250b99902131" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Identity &amp; tiers</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">3 · Signing identity: what a signature proves, tier by tier</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Open <Text bold="true">Help ▸ Signing identity…</Text>. The top line says who this computer signs as and at which <Text bold="true">tier</Text>. A tier is an honest statement of what the key mechanism resists — never of who the person is:</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Level 0 (claimed): a typed name, no key. Proves the contents did not change since signing; proves nothing about who signed. Every record made before this section was Level 0 — the signature pages say so.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Level 1 (local-passphrase): a key file on the computer, unlocked by a passphrase per signature. What the cast uses here.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Level 2 (dpapi-cng): a key bound to the Windows account, no prompt; what SafeSuite's own commits sign with.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Level 3 (tpm-hello): a TPM-held key gated by Windows Hello per signature.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Level 4 (hardware-token) and Level 5 (pki-cert): an external token, or a certificate from your personal store (enroll one and it reads third-party-vetted when the chain is trusted).</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph>The second axis, <Text bold="true">vetting</Text>, says who checked that the key belongs to the named person: self-enrolled (nobody), site-manager-vetted (the trust root attested it), third-party-vetted (a certificate authority). Both axes are written on every signature at the moment it is made and never upgraded afterwards: an old Level 0 record stays Level 0 forever.</Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> as Elena, open a signed document (2_Concept/HARA.strdoc) and scroll to the signature page at its foot: each signature names its tier and vetting, and the caveat below the signatures is the one owed to the weakest tier present. Then, in the same dialog, <Text bold="true">Session unlock</Text>: one passphrase prompt now, and a time-limited key issued by yours signs save receipts and SafeSuite's commits for the next hours without asking again — every such record says 'session key'.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="522fcec1-c966-5104-bb9e-1185c16ad14a" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>The policy</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">4 · The policy editor: roles, people, requirements, keys</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Open <Text bold="true">Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Governance policy…</Text> as Elena. Four tabs:</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>People: every grant. Elena and Ravi are Owners; Callum's grant shows 'Valid until 2026-09-15'; 田中 優子's carve-out excludes Model/AI-Demos; Νίκος's row carries a minimum-tier override of 2. The Find box narrows the grid; Copy copies one folder's assignments to another.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Roles: the capability set behind each role name. Add a role, tick capabilities; 'Everything' is the Owner's floor and cannot be delegated or waived away.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Requirements: per capability, the minimum tier and the process rules — Signers, Not the author, One must hold role, Pending timeout (h) — plus the project-wide settings: Signed commits, 'Delegations last at most N days', and the CLMS box (row 16).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Keys: every enrolled key with tier, vetting, CLMS link and status; the trust root's tools: Vet key, Revoke key, Make trust root.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Save and sign</Text> writes .safesuite/policy.json signed with the saver's key and records the change with its diff in the activity log (Policy changed, Grant given, Grant revoked). <Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> become Tomasz, open the editor: everything is read-only and Save is refused — only a holder of 'Edit the policy' can change it, and the refusal names who holds it. Become Elena, add a role 'Reader+' and save: the log gains a Policy changed entry, and the policy history (row 21) gains a version.</Paragraph><Paragraph>Three versions are already on record: v1 (adoption by Ravi, the whole team named), v2 (the process rules and minimum tiers), v3 (Callum's window, the carve-out, the tier override, the 30-day delegation ceiling). Absent policy means unchanged behaviour: a project without policy.json works exactly as before governance existed.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="eae90801-b835-50a9-a69d-6d985a28e817" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Refusals</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">5 · A refusal is visible, worded, and recorded</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>The policy is asked when a person tries, not when the menu is drawn: a governed action you may not take stays in the menu, and choosing it shows why it was refused instead of silently doing nothing. <Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> become Tomasz, open 4_Hardware/Reliability_Metrics.strdoc, Document ▸ Approve…: "'Approve documents' needs to be granted to you for … It is held by … Ask … for access" — and the offer to request a waiver (row 8). The refusal lands in the activity log as <Text bold="true">Refused by policy</Text> (access.denied), often the most informative entry there.</Paragraph><Paragraph>Also try: become Callum and Document ▸ Approve… on 2_Concept/HARA.strdoc — refused by the four-eyes rule first (he last saved it, so he cannot sign it), and by the policy right after: he holds no approving right anywhere (row 9).</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="81835a0b-34d3-534c-873b-c3a549ebb3b2" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Effective policy</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">6 · The effective-policy inspector: what holds at a place</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Grants add up, folder by folder, so the question 'who can approve here?' has a computed answer. Right-click <Text bold="true">5_Software</Text> in the project explorer ▸ <Text bold="true">Show Effective Policy Here…</Text>: every capability, who holds it at that place and through which grant, the required tier, and — new in this section — how long a windowed right has left.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Approve documents at Model/5_Software: Elena (Owner), Ravi (Owner), Greta (Assessor), Claire (Approver in Model/5_Software).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Edit documents at Model/5_Software: Callum's row reads 'Author in Model/5_Software until 2026-09-15 (lapses in N days)', and the Lapsing soon column and the summary line above the grid list it while fewer than fourteen days remain.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Change the Place box to Model/AI-Demos/Minutes_2026-03-14.md and Inspect: 田中 優子 no longer holds Read documents — her carve-out.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Project-wide capabilities (Create baselines, Sign baselines, Edit the policy…) come only from grants without a folder scope, wherever you ask.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="8d913ad0-644a-5174-95a4-88681b11e37c" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Temporary access</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">7 · Temporary access: a grant with an end, and the moment it ends</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>A contractor engaged for two weeks, a probation, a cover for a colleague on leave: ordinary, planned situations, so the grant itself carries the window (People tab, 'Valid from' and 'Valid until'). When the window closes, the grant is simply one contributor fewer — not an exception, not a special state — and the lapse is written down when it happens.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Callum Reid: Author in Model/5_Software until 2026-09-15. The inspector counts down (row 6); the policy still lists him afterwards, it just no longer counts.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Дмитрий Соколов: Author in Model/6_Verification from 1 June to 31 August 2026. The window had already closed when governance was adopted, so the first Verify all seals wrote 'Grant expired' (grant.expired) into the activity log — the log shows when access actually ended, not only that it would.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Try it as Elena: give Tomasz 'Valid until' tomorrow in the People tab and save; inspect Model/4_Hardware; then set it to yesterday and save again — the next Verify all seals logs the lapse.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="7317a9b3-4f55-51c5-bc9f-562c1561ab1e" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Waivers</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">8 · Waivers: a one-off exception, requested, decided, and worn on the record</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Sometimes the right person is away and the work cannot wait. A <Text bold="true">waiver</Text> is a narrow, bounded exception to the policy: one capability, one scope, single-use or with an expiry, requested by the person who needs it and granted or denied by a holder of 'Grant waivers' — never by themselves. Every record made under a waiver says so, forever: the anomaly is worn, not hidden.</Paragraph><Paragraph>Open <Text bold="true">Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Policy waivers…</Text>. Three are on record:</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>W-20260905-315f — Paolo Ferraro, Approve documents for Model/6_Verification/Validation_Report.strdoc, single-use: granted by Elena ('One-off for the release board…'), then used. Open the Validation_Report: its signature page reads 'Reviewer Paolo Ferraro … under waiver W-20260905-315f'. The report's 'RECORDS MADE UNDER A WAIVER' section lists it too.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>W-20260905-a6b6 — Callum Reid, Sign baselines, project-wide: denied by Elena ('A contractor never signs a release; the assessor does'). A denied request can be re-requested; the denial stays on record.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>W-20260905-18d0 — Tomasz Kowal, Approve documents for Model/4_Hardware/Reliability_Metrics.strdoc, valid a week: pending. Become Elena, select it, write a reason, Grant or Deny.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Try the whole loop:</Text> become Tomasz, Document ▸ Approve… on Reliability_Metrics — refused — answer Yes to 'Request a waiver for this?', fill the reason and bound; become Elena, grant it in Policy waivers…; become Tomasz again and approve: the approval carries the waiver id. A waiver for the very action reduces the required signatures to one; the author rule still applies. Revoke ends a granted waiver early, signed.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="4f43906a-b45a-58b2-9993-4772054db5b2" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Four-eyes rules</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">9 · Process rules: four eyes, not the author, one of them an assessor</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>A signature is only as good as the process around it. The Requirements tab lets a capability demand more than one person ('Signers'), refuse the document's own last author ('Not the author'), require one of the signers to hold a named role ('One must hold role'), and withdraw an incomplete approval after a timeout. Here: <Text bold="true">Approve documents</Text> needs 2 signers, never the author, within 720 hours; <Text bold="true">Sign baselines</Text> needs 2 signers, one holding Assessor.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>2_Concept/HARA.strdoc: Approved by two signatures — Elena Vidal (Approver) and Greta Hoffmann (Assessor), both Level 1 — after Callum, who last saved it, sent it for review. Its June approval (Level 0, one signature) had been invalidated by the HZ-07 edit; the new one is what the signature page shows.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>3_System/Technical_Safety_Concept.strdoc: In review with 1 of 2 signatures (Elena's). Henrik sent it and, as its author, cannot sign it.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>5_Software/Software_Architecture.strdoc: In review with 1 of 2 signatures — Paolo's, acting under Claire's delegation (row 10).</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Pending signatures…</Text> lists every approval and seal waiting for more signatures, so nothing sits forgotten. <Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> become Greta and Document ▸ Approve… the TSC: the confirmation states the progress (one signature collected, yours completes it); after your passphrase the document is Approved and the log reads 'Approved … (2 of 2 signatures)'. Try it as Henrik first to see the author refusal in words. The first signer of a pending approval can take their signature back (the record stays In review with no signatures); an approval left pending past the timeout is withdrawn at the next verify.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="e2f5f72f-0e7e-5fa9-a453-2814ceeae8bb" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Delegation</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">10 · Delegation: lend what you hold, for a while, at or below your own scope</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Whoever holds a capability may lend it — no separate 'may delegate' right, no policy edit — for a bounded time, to a named person, within their own scope. The lent right is re-derived from the delegator's <Text bold="true">current</Text> rights every time it is used: revoke the delegator's grant and the delegation stops working the same moment, whatever its own window says. A delegation can never reach the Owner's floor (Edit the policy, Everything).</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>D-20260905-76b7 — Claire Moreau lends Approve documents in Model/5_Software to Paolo Ferraro until 2026-09-19 ('Annual leave 8-19 September…'). Paolo used it: the Software_Architecture's first signature reads 'acting under D-20260905-76b7'.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>D-20260905-b2b4 — Henrik Larsen lent Edit documents in Model/3_System to Callum for a week; Elena revoked it ('Contractor edits stay in 5_Software…'). Revocation is a signed record, same shape as a key's or a waiver's.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>The policy caps a delegation at 30 days ('Delegations last at most'); anything longer goes through the policy editor, where it belongs.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Where:</Text> Help ▸ Signing identity… ▸ <Text bold="true">Delegate my access…</Text> (your own rights, place by place, a person, days, a reason), or Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ <Text bold="true">Delegations…</Text> — the same window lists every delegation in the project with its state; its delegator or an Owner can revoke it. <Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> become Henrik, lend Approve documents in Model/3_System to Tomasz for 5 days; become Tomasz, approve the TSC: the signature says under whose delegation. Then become Elena and remove Henrik's Approver role: Tomasz's delegation dies with it, and the verifier re-derives the earlier signature as legitimate — it was covered when it was made.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="16c0b748-1c65-5356-870c-585e75b7cd14" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Seals with rules</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">11 · Sealing a baseline under the seal rules</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>A baseline seals the exact bytes of every file in scope under a manifest hash; a signature over that hash is the seal. Under the policy, sealing is a governed action with its own process rule: two signers, one of whom holds Assessor. Baseline <Text bold="true">v1.3 - Governance</Text> (whole project) was sealed by Elena Vidal (Approver) and Greta Hoffmann (Assessor) at the end of this section; it is the current state and verifies green.</Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Where:</Text> View ▸ Versions ▸ Baselines tab: Create Baseline…, Sign / Approve, Verify Integrity, Export Evidence Pack…; Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Create baseline…. <Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> become Ravi (Project manager: may create and sign, holds no Assessor role), create a baseline over 6_Verification and sign it: 'Pending (1 of 2)'. Sign again as Elena: refused in words — every set of signers must include someone holding Assessor, and this would be signature 2 of 2 without one (an Owner holds every capability, not every role name). Sign as Greta: sealed. Pending signatures… shows the seal while it waits; Verify all seals reports a breach of the rule as a VIOLATION, never as a green seal.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="e6a293fc-9dc1-5694-9bca-f7c6a28713de" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Verify all seals</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">12 · Verify all seals: one command re-derives everything</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Verify all seals</Text> (or safesuite-verify on the command line, row 20) checks, from the files alone: every baseline's files and signatures; every governed document's state, receipts and approvals; whether each signer held the right at the time of signing under the policy in force then, or a waiver or delegation that covered it; the policy's signature and every version's legitimacy; the trust records and the root chain; waivers and delegations; commit signatures; changes to governed documents commit by commit; and the activity log's hash chain. Every run is itself logged (Verified).</Paragraph><Paragraph>Governance/Verify_all_seals.txt is the report as it stood on <Text bold="true">2026-09-05</Text>. Read it section by section; the findings it lists are <Text bold="true">expected and intended</Text>:</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>BASELINES: v1.3 - Governance verified. v0.9, v1.0, v1.1 and v1.2 are BROKEN — each names exactly the files changed after it was sealed (the HARA refinement, the role views, the September maintenance wave, the SBOM and Jira work). That is a seal doing its job: a seal is a statement about bytes at a moment, and later legitimate work breaks it by definition; the newest seal is the one expected to be green.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>CHANGES TO GOVERNED DOCUMENTS: 'Governance was adopted at commit …; changes before it were made under no policy and are not judged.' The September maintenance wave was committed by scripts, not saved through SafeSuite, so it carries no save receipts — history before the policy is named as unjudged, never mistaken for verified.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Everything after adoption verifies: every commit signed, every changed document with a receipt, every approval covered by a grant, waiver or delegation, the policy's three versions each legitimate, the root chain continuous, the activity log's chain intact.</Paragraph></ListItem></List><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> open 2_Concept/HARA.strdoc with Notepad, change a word, save, and run Verify all seals: v1.3 turns BROKEN naming HARA.strdoc, the HARA's approval is invalidated ('approval invalidated by a later edit'), and the document is flagged 'changed outside SafeSuite … attributed to nobody' until someone saves it in SafeSuite again. Undo the edit (or git checkout the file) and verify: green again — nothing was rewritten, the seal just tells the truth about the bytes.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="9a9c7dfb-ece9-54e9-9360-c2e080e3d3cc" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Receipts</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">13 · Save receipts: every save leaves a signed note</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Every save SafeSuite makes leaves a small signed record beside the document (.safesuite/documents/…json): who saved this exact content, when, with which key. A commit that changes a governed document must carry that receipt; a change committed around SafeSuite — by a script, a text editor, a merge tool — is a finding ('changed without a matching save record'), and a refused push where a hook or a required check enforces it (row 19).</Paragraph><Paragraph>Here every document touched under governance was re-saved in SafeSuite by its author before the reviews began (the guide itself was saved by Elena), so the report's CHANGES TO GOVERNED DOCUMENTS section lists each change with 'saved by … (Level 1 (local-passphrase), …), receipt verified'. Level 1 keys ask for a passphrase, and a save happens all the time, so a locked Level 1 identity leaves <Text bold="true">claimed</Text> (Level 0) receipts — honest, and visible; unlock a session (row 3) and receipts sign with the session key instead.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="bcbd6d72-7429-57a4-b479-8af160e1c64a" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Signed commits</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">14 · Signed commits: the history itself is signed</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Every commit in this section is SSH-signed with its author's key — the same key that signs approvals — and the report's COMMITS section verifies each against the project's trust records ('of the last 200 commits: N verified'). git log --show-signature verifies them too, once Help ▸ Signing identity ▸ Configure git points git at the projected allowed_signers file under .safesuite/trust/.</Paragraph><Paragraph>The policy's <Text bold="true">Signed commits</Text> setting (Requirements tab) is 'off' here on purpose: SafeSuite's own commits sign themselves only with a key that opens without a prompt (Level 2, or a Level 1/3 session key), and a demonstration should not fail its first checkpoint because nobody unlocked a session. Set it to 'governed' as Elena and verify: every unsigned commit touching a governed path from then on is an UNSIGNED finding, judged under the policy in force when it was made — earlier commits are not re-judged.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="92ca8093-c444-5caf-81c3-973c0590a1f7" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Trust root &amp; keys</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">15 · Keys, the trust root, vetting and revocation</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>A person's key is enrolled in a project the first time they sign something there, with a self-signature that proves possession. The first keyed enrollment becomes the project's <Text bold="true">trust root</Text> — the key that vouches for others (vetting) and can revoke keys — the way the creator of a repository is its implicit owner. The root can be handed on, signed by the outgoing root: root.json is a chain, and Verify all seals checks its continuity against history.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Keys tab of the policy editor: Ravi enrolled first (he adopted the policy), then handed the root to Elena ('Trust root handed on' in the log; the report's 'Trust root chain' line names both, and checks that every hand-over recorded in history is in the current chain).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Vetting: Elena attested Henrik's, Greta's, Paolo's and Claire's keys as site-manager-vetted, each attestation citing what she checked; Claire's cites her CLMS seat (row 16). Tomasz, Callum and Ravi are self-enrolled. From the attestation on, their signatures record the vetting; earlier ones keep what they had.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Дмитрий Соколов's key was revoked by Elena the day governance was adopted ('Contract ended on 31 August 2026…'): signatures it made before stand, later ones would not verify. Revocation is a signed record; only the holder, the key that issued it, or the root can make one.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Try it as Elena: select Tomasz's key, Vet key — 'site-manager-vetted (by Elena Vidal)'. Then Make trust root on Greta's key: the chain gains a link signed by you; Verify all seals reports the hand-over as legitimate. (become.cmd -Restore leaves the project's records as they are; reset.cmd restores the pristine clone.)</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="33c3f22f-9db7-5ad3-a1d7-d96ccfab3aed" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>CLMS link</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">16 · CLMS account link: evidence of who you are, never a dependency</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>The one verified identity anywhere in the product is the CLMS licence sign-in — and CLMS being down is exactly the failure this design must survive. So the link is a record, not a lookup: <Text bold="true">Help ▸ Signing identity… ▸ Link to CLMS account…</Text> makes one interactive sign-in, reads the seats CLMS says are assigned to that account, and writes a statement signed by your key on your identity: account, server, seats, when. The password serves that one sign-in; the token is used once and not kept; nothing ever consults CLMS afterwards. A licence refreshing on a computer proves the computer is entitled, not who sits at it, and is deliberately never read.</Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Elena's and Claire's keys carry a link (Keys tab, CLMS column): 'CLMS account claire.moreau@… on clms.cynoss.net, 1 seat(s): AGER-SDE-04'. The link is bound to the key and to the seats by its signature; it can be withdrawn by the holder or the root, signed.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Elena's attestation of Claire's key cites the link, so Claire's vetting reads 'site-manager-vetted (by Elena Vidal), citing CLMS account …'.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>The Requirements tab's box 'A self-reported CLMS seat link counts as site-manager-vetted' is off: until a governance service counter-signs such links they are the holder's own statement, and the reader always sees which basis a vetting rests on.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Try it: with any account, Link to CLMS account… against a server that is not there — 'CLMS at … is unreachable … Nothing changed: your signing identity works exactly as before'. That sentence is the whole design.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="f37baf76-a484-5f67-b31c-e1891e541f86" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Activity log</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">17 · The activity log: hash-chained, narrated, complete</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Activity log</Text> (also the Versions pane's Activity log tab). Every governed event is an entry — Governance enabled, Policy changed, Grant given, Key enrolled, Key vetted, Key revoked, Trust root handed on, Waiver requested / granted / denied / used, Access delegated, Delegation revoked, Sent for review, Signature added, Approved, Baseline sealed, Baseline signed, Grant expired, Refused by policy, Verified — each carrying the actor, the object, the evidence reference (a commit id, a manifest hash) and a hash of the previous entry. Verify all seals ends with 'ACTIVITY LOG: N entries, chain intact'; an edited or truncated log breaks the chain and is reported as such.</Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> filter the log by user (Elena Vidal) or by action; then open .safesuite/audit/log.jsonl in a text editor, delete one middle line, and verify: 'chain broken at N'. Put the line back: intact again. The log is append-only text under version control, so its own history is in git too.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="84ca201f-4021-50de-9e91-d1098ab83139" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Evidence &amp; signature pages</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">18 · Signature pages and the evidence pack</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Every reviewed document ends with a <Text bold="true">signature page</Text>: each signature with its role, name, date, tier and vetting, the waiver or delegation it was made under when it was, and — below — the caveat owed to the weakest tier present (a Level 0 page says in so many words that it does not prove who signed). The <Text bold="true">evidence pack</Text> (Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Export evidence pack…, or the Baselines tab) zips a baseline's manifest, the byte-copied records, the activity log narration and a certificate whose caveat is tier-dependent: the same honesty on paper as on screen. PDF exports (Document ▸ Export as PDF…) carry the lifecycle and the signatures on their cover.</Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> export the evidence pack of v1.3 - Governance as Greta (Assessor: may export evidence) and open the certificate; then try as Callum: refused — Export evidence is project-wide and his grant is scoped to 5_Software.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="b7db8329-afdd-5d24-8a19-c6268a77d0c6" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Hooks &amp; servers</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">19 · Team server, hook kit and branch protection: from report to refusal</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Everything above is <Text bold="true">detective</Text>: findings are reported and logged, nothing is prevented — that is the design's first principle, because a colleague can always edit a file with Notepad. To also <Text bold="true">refuse</Text> a push that leaves findings, the server has to run the check. <Text bold="true">Assessment ▸ Attestation &amp; signing ▸ Team server settings…</Text> names the tier this repository is on (GitHub: a hosted service, so 'enforcement needs a required check'), can <Text bold="true">Check enforcement</Text> through the GitHub API with the stored access token (administration rights needed to read branch protection), and <Text bold="true">Save hook kit…</Text> exports the four files: a pre-receive hook for a self-hosted git server, and CI templates for GitHub Actions, GitLab CI and Azure Pipelines, each running safesuite-verify and failing on any finding.</Paragraph><Paragraph>The kit as exported is under Governance/CI/ (kept there rather than under .github/ so that this demonstration repository does not start running a workflow by itself); its README says what to do with each file. On a shared-folder server nothing can refuse anything, and the dialog says so.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="15e628fe-264d-51f9-9005-17712628d02d" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Command line</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">20 · safesuite-verify: the same check from a terminal, a hook or a pipeline</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>safesuite-verify.exe ships next to SafeSuite.exe and is the verifier without the window: </Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>safesuite-verify C:\Demo\SafeSuite-Showcase — the working tree, the report as text (or --json).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>safesuite-verify --git &lt;repo&gt; --ref &lt;commit&gt; [--since &lt;commit&gt;] — a commit as it was, materialised in a temporary folder; with --since, only the commits of a push range are judged.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>safesuite-verify --hook — the pre-receive mode the hook kit installs: reads the pushed ranges from stdin and exits non-zero on findings or rewritten history.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Help ▸ Signing identity ▸ Configure git makes it git's gpg.ssh.program too, so git log --show-signature verifies SafeSuite's commit signatures against .safesuite/trust/allowed_signers.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="83509949-3925-5352-a743-6036d1c9b9c5" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Moving documents</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">21 · Moving a document across scopes, and what changes</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>Rights follow folders, so moving a document changes who may do what to it. An in-app move that changes a document's coverage asks first, listing who loses and who gains what ('Claire loses Approve documents; Paolo loses Approve documents (delegated)…'), and the acknowledged move is logged (Moved across a policy scope). <Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> as Elena, drag 5_Software/Software_Architecture.strdoc into 6_Verification and read the confirmation; press Cancel.</Paragraph><Paragraph>Records follow the document: a document renamed or moved inside SafeSuite keeps its lifecycle record and approvals (the approval is over the content, not the path); one moved outside SafeSuite is found by its identity and reported as RELOCATED until the record is moved with it.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="4ba07e4a-bdd8-58a9-9e49-620e9bcefcfd" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Policy history</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">22 · Policy history: every record is judged under the policy of its time</Text></Paragraph><Paragraph>The policy is a signed file in git, so its every version is on record: 'Policy history: 3 version(s) on record, governance enabled 2026-09-05', each version judged legitimate (saved by a holder of Edit the policy under the version before it). A record is checked against the policy <Text bold="true">in force when it was made</Text> — never against today's: a grant removed later does not make an earlier sign-off wrong, and history is never rewritten.</Paragraph><Paragraph><Text bold="true">Try it:</Text> become Elena, remove Greta's grant entirely and save; verify: the HARA's approval by Greta still verifies and is not flagged (she held the right when she signed), the policy history has four versions, and the effective policy at Model/2_Concept no longer lists her. Records made before the first version are judged against it with a question mark ('VIOLATION?') rather than an assertion, because the policy of their time is unknown.</Paragraph></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="2ac2e423-71e4-525b-a4ac-11ea3eed7ef5" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Not here</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">23 · What is deliberately not in this demonstration</Text></Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>Level 4 hardware tokens (FIDO2/PIV) — designed, waiting for hardware; Level 5 certificates work today with a certificate from your own personal store (Help ▸ Signing identity ▸ enroll, tier pki-cert).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>A governance directory service (company-wide people, role templates, counter-signed records): designed as a permanent sibling to CLMS, not started; nothing here depends on it.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>Prevention on GitHub: the required check is exported, not installed, so pushes to this repository are not refused — the findings are reported and logged instead. Install the workflow in your own fork to see a pull request refused.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>The maintenance wave of early September was committed by scripts, so it has no save receipts; it predates the policy and is reported as unjudged — an honest report of a real history, kept as it was.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
  <Item id="96b4a6ec-63fd-5dc3-8084-0ea5a8f83853" type="Default">
    <Attribute name="Section">
      <Value>Reset</Value>
    </Attribute>
    <Attribute name="Contents">
      <Value><Paragraph><Text bold="true" font-size="+16%">24 · Putting things back</Text></Paragraph><List type="Bullet"><ListItem><Paragraph>become.cmd -Restore returns this computer to your own signing identity (or to Level 0 if you had none).</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>reset.cmd (project root) restores the bench to the pristine clone from GitHub: every record laid here comes back exactly as sealed; everything you tried is gone.</Paragraph></ListItem><ListItem><Paragraph>The identity packs stay in the repository: anyone who clones it can become the cast. Their keys sign nothing outside this project's trust records.</Paragraph></ListItem></List></Value>
    </Attribute>
  </Item>
</StructuredDocument>
